$academyConfig['rich_lessons']['*']['solar-system-planetary-science']=[
 space_lesson('A Solar System Built to Scale','Scale models','Distances and sizes in space differ by enormous factors. A useful model states its scale and keeps diameter and distance consistent.','Create a scale that turns one astronomical unit into one metre, then place Earth and Mars.','A model is useful because it preserves a chosen relationship, not because it includes every detail.','If 1 AU equals 1 metre, Earth is 1 metre from the Sun and Mars averages about 1.52 metres away.','scale'),
 space_lesson('The Sun: Our Local Star','Solar energy','The Sun contains almost all Solar System mass. Fusion in its core converts hydrogen into helium and releases energy that moves outward as radiation.','Trace energy from the solar core to light reaching Earth.','Gravity compresses the core enough for fusion; outward pressure and inward gravity keep the Sun stable.','Sunlight takes about eight minutes to cross the average Sun–Earth distance.','sun'),
 space_lesson('Rocky Worlds','Terrestrial planets','Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars have solid surfaces but very different atmospheres, temperatures, and geological histories.','Compare two rocky planets and identify the evidence behind one difference.','Similar starting materials can produce different worlds through size, atmosphere, water, impacts, and internal heat.','Venus is hotter than Mercury because its thick carbon-dioxide atmosphere traps heat efficiently.','world-compare'),
 space_lesson('The Gas Giants','Jupiter and Saturn','Jupiter and Saturn are dominated by hydrogen and helium, have deep atmospheres, ring systems, and large families of moons.','Design a probe measurement that could investigate a giant planet atmosphere.','A gas giant has no ordinary solid surface; pressure and temperature rise with depth.','Jupiter’s Great Red Spot is a long-lived atmospheric storm larger than Earth.','world-compare'),
 space_lesson('The Ice Giants','Uranus and Neptune','Uranus and Neptune contain larger proportions of water, ammonia, and methane-rich material than Jupiter and Saturn.','Explain why “ice giant” describes composition rather than a simple frozen surface.','Planet categories summarize composition and structure, but every world has distinct weather and history.','Methane absorbs red light and contributes to the blue appearance of both planets.','world-compare'),
 space_lesson('Dwarf Planets and Classification','Scientific classification','A dwarf planet orbits the Sun and is rounded by gravity but has not cleared its orbital neighbourhood.','Classify a fictional world using the formal criteria.','Scientific categories change when evidence and definitions improve; a category is a tool, not a ranking.','Pluto is geologically complex even though its classification changed in 2006.','classify'),
 space_lesson('Moons as Worlds','Natural satellites','Moons can have oceans, atmospheres, volcanoes, magnetic interactions, and evidence of active geology.','Rank Europa, Titan, and Enceladus for a chosen science mission.','A small body can be a complex world when tidal heating and chemistry provide energy.','Europa likely holds a global salty ocean beneath an icy crust.','moon-mission'),
 space_lesson('Asteroids, Comets, and Meteors','Small bodies','Asteroids are rocky or metallic bodies; comets are rich in volatile material; a meteor is the streak produced in an atmosphere.','Build a diagram connecting meteoroid, meteor, and meteorite.','Small bodies preserve clues from early Solar System formation and also create impact hazards.','A meteoroid becomes a meteor while glowing in an atmosphere and a meteorite if material reaches the ground.','classify'),
 space_lesson('Gravity Writes the Orbits','Orbital motion','An orbit is continuous free fall: gravity bends an object’s forward motion around another body. Elliptical orbits change speed and distance.','Predict what happens when an orbiting object gains or loses speed.','Objects do not need continuous thrust to remain in orbit, but they do need the right sideways velocity.','A satellite in low Earth orbit falls toward Earth while Earth’s curved surface falls away beneath it.','orbit'),
 space_lesson('Planetary Mission Design Lab','Scientific missions','A mission connects one answerable question to an instrument, destination, trajectory, power source, communication plan, and risk budget.','Draft a robotic mission with one question, three instruments, and one major tradeoff.','Successful exploration is focused: every kilogram, watt, minute, and dollar must support the mission goal.','A Europa flyby can sample many regions with less radiation exposure than a long-duration orbiter.','mission'),
