$academyConfig['rich_lessons']['*']['stars-light-galaxies']=[
 space_lesson('Light Is Cosmic Information','Electromagnetic spectrum','Different wavelengths reveal temperature, composition, motion, magnetic fields, and hidden structures.','Match radio, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, and gamma observations to useful targets.','Astronomy is largely the science of decoding light and other signals that reach our instruments.','Infrared observations can see through dust that blocks visible light.','spectrum'),
 space_lesson('Stars Are Born in Clouds','Star formation','Cold molecular clouds collapse into dense cores. A protostar forms, heats, and eventually begins hydrogen fusion.','Order the stages from cloud to main-sequence star.','Gravity builds a star; fusion marks the start of its long stable life.','The Orion Nebula contains gas, dust, young stars, and forming planetary systems.','star-life'),
 space_lesson('Reading a Star','Stellar properties','Colour relates to surface temperature, spectra reveal elements, brightness depends on luminosity and distance, and motion shifts spectral lines.','Use colour and spectral clues to compare two stars.','One observation rarely tells the whole story; astronomers combine measurements and models.','A blue star is generally hotter at its surface than a red star.','star-color'),
 space_lesson('Main-Sequence Fusion','Stellar stability','Main-sequence stars fuse hydrogen while pressure from hot plasma balances inward gravity. Mass controls temperature, brightness, and lifetime.','Predict which of two stars uses its fuel faster.','More massive stars have more fuel but burn it disproportionately faster.','The Sun has remained on the main sequence for about 4.6 billion years.','star-life'),
 space_lesson('The Future of Sun-like Stars','Low-mass evolution','A Sun-like star expands into a red giant, sheds outer layers, and leaves a dense white dwarf.','Build a lifecycle diagram for a low-mass star.','A star’s initial mass is the strongest predictor of its evolution.','The Sun will not explode as a core-collapse supernova.','star-life'),
 space_lesson('Massive Stars and Supernovae','High-mass evolution','Massive stars fuse successively heavier elements until the core can no longer release energy through fusion, triggering collapse and explosion.','Explain how supernovae distribute elements into space.','Many elements in planets and bodies were forged in stars or stellar explosions.','Core-collapse supernovae can leave neutron stars or black holes.','star-life'),
 space_lesson('Stellar Remnants','White dwarfs, neutron stars, black holes','Compact remnants pack mass into small volumes. Their gravity, rotation, magnetic fields, and surrounding matter reveal their presence.','Compare the evidence used to detect each remnant.','Black holes are observed through measurable effects, not imagined as cosmic vacuum cleaners.','Pulsars are rotating neutron stars whose beams sweep across Earth.','remnants'),
 space_lesson('Our Milky Way','Galactic structure','The Milky Way contains a disk, bulge, bar, spiral structure, halo, dark matter, gas, dust, and hundreds of billions of stars.','Locate the Solar System in a labelled Milky Way model.','We observe our galaxy from inside its disk, so mapping it requires multiple wavelengths and distance methods.','The Sun lies in the Orion Spur, far from the galactic centre.','galaxy'),
 space_lesson('A Universe of Galaxies','Galaxy diversity','Spiral, elliptical, and irregular galaxies reflect different histories of star formation, gas, interaction, and merger.','Classify sample galaxies by visible structure and state the uncertainty.','Appearance is evidence, but classification should not pretend every object fits perfectly.','Galaxy collisions can trigger star formation without individual stars usually colliding.','galaxy'),
 space_lesson('Cosmic Distance Ladder Project','Measuring distance','Parallax, standard candles, redshift, and other methods overlap to build a ladder from nearby stars to distant galaxies.','Create a one-page guide showing which distance method works at each scale.','Independent overlapping methods let astronomers check calibration and uncertainty.','Parallax measures the apparent shift of a nearby star as Earth moves around the Sun.','distance'),
