 space_lesson('Gravity as Geometry','Spacetime','General relativity describes mass and energy curving spacetime, while objects follow the resulting paths.','Compare Newton’s useful approximation with a relativistic case.','Models can be valid in different ranges; deeper theory does not make every simpler model useless.','GPS timing requires relativistic corrections.','gravity'),
 space_lesson('An Expanding Universe','Cosmic expansion','On large scales, distant galaxies generally recede faster with greater distance as space expands. Bound systems do not simply stretch with it.','Plot a simplified distance–recession relationship.','Expansion is not an explosion from a central point into empty space.','Every large-scale observer sees other distant galaxies receding.','expansion'),
 space_lesson('Evidence for the Hot Big Bang','Cosmic history','Expansion, the cosmic microwave background, and primordial element abundances support a hot, dense early universe.','Match each observation to the prediction it supports.','The Big Bang model describes cosmic evolution from an early hot state, not what caused existence.','The cosmic microwave background is cooled relic radiation from the early universe.','evidence'),
 space_lesson('Dark Matter','Invisible mass','Galaxy rotation, gravitational lensing, clusters, and cosmic structure indicate more gravitating matter than visible material provides.','Compare competing explanations and identify the measurement each must fit.','Dark matter is inferred from multiple independent gravitational effects.','Lensing maps mass even when that mass emits little or no light.','dark-matter'),
 space_lesson('Dark Energy','Accelerated expansion','Observations of distant supernovae and cosmic structure indicate that expansion is accelerating. The physical explanation remains an open problem.','Separate the observation from proposed explanations.','Naming an unknown effect does not mean its nature is understood.','Type Ia supernovae helped reveal accelerated expansion.','expansion'),
